\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\taxon{Definition}
\title{Selected-token surprisal}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{entropy}

\p{For a sampled token #{A_t} at public history #{H_t}, its
\strong{selected-token surprisal} is}

##{
  I_t=-\log \pi_t(A_t\mid H_t).
}

\p{This is the selected action's self-information; compare the information
measure in \citet{Pt. I, Sec. 6}{shannon1948mathematical}. It is a random
variable, whereas local entropy is its conditional expectation before the
action is sampled.}

\p{Low-probability-token analyses and entropy-shaped RLVR methods often select
or reweight positions using quantities related to #{I_t}. Such a selector is
not equivalent to preserving global entropy. Later empirical trees can attach
the exact selector from each source without changing this definition.}
